    {
      "question": "e-4724",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/problem",
      "old_sha256": "ad4287612950df5c392fe2e3a2100decb999c361db0020d09f51565476286133",
      "new_sha256": "e6ca92441b8227188d32ed5207104e52e17d0eac25eeb227f5dc1aa1add09c48",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-4724",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/problem",
          "value": "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/example-4724.json",
          "file_sha256": "d2b4bfcdd3824180497413575fc8ed97f84884adca0406f94bcaf9b92947c812"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 1555,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 1556
    },
    {
      "question": "e-4724",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "8c4488d0cc4a0f0e81b3a0a580d098bee9780fbbca3ee3455295f46693e1af03",
      "new_sha256": "8a641f3d99d3b3122fedd899eaf918e8f3bad0149b8dc61766e702736faf8ad2",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-4724",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/247c0a29294e61735b969d769d8ba3794c0b5e816c3968a8db087740eeb925d8.png)",
          "category": "ma_capture",
